Below is a step‐by‐step decomposition of the mission description into a hierarchical goal model for a multi‐robot food delivery and dish retrieval system. In this design, we break the mission into a top‐level goal (G1) that must achieve two independent but essential outcomes: (1) food delivery from the kitchen to the inpatient room and (2) retrieval of dirty dishes from the room. We then further decompose each branch according to the alternatives and operational details described.

──────────────────────────────
Step 1. Top‐Level Goal (G1)
──────────────────────────────
• G1 is an “Achieve” goal because it specifies a target state: the inpatient room should have the delivered food and the dirty dishes must be retrieved.
• It has two children: food delivery (G2) and dish retrieval (G3). Since both are essential but can be executed independently, we use a parallel execution operator (“#”) for G1.  
• Justification: The mission description provides two separate objectives that do not depend on each other for execution.

──────────────────────────────
Step 2. Food Delivery Branch (G2)
──────────────────────────────
G2 is also an “Achieve” goal with the target state that food is delivered to the inpatient room. The description offers two alternatives:
 ○ Direct table delivery (G2.1)  
 ○ Tray retrieval (G2.2)

Because the mission allows either method, we use an OR decomposition for G2.

• G2.1: Direct Table Delivery  
 – Type: Perform goal (an action executed by the robot using a special manipulation skill).  
 – Runtime: Leaf node (“-”)  
 – Relation: This is one alternative under the OR decomposition of G2.

• G2.2: Tray Retrieval  
 – Type: Achieve goal (target state: food retrieved from the tray).  
 – This branch is further decomposed sequentially:
  • G2.2.1: Query Inpatient Record  
   – Type: Query goal (to retrieve information about retrieval capability).  
   – Enquired Information: Inpatient, companion, nurse, or robot availability status.  
   – Runtime: Leaf (“-”)  
  • G2.2.2: Execute Tray Retrieval  
   – Type: Perform goal (action to retrieve food from the tray).  
   – Runtime: Leaf (“-”)  
 – Relation: The two sub-goals (query and execute) are linked by an AND relationship and must be executed sequentially (“;”) because the query result informs the retrieval action.

──────────────────────────────
Step 3. Dish Retrieval Branch (G3)
──────────────────────────────
G3 is an “Achieve” goal with the target state that dirty dishes are retrieved from the inpatient room. However, before dishes can be retrieved, the door must be opened. Thus, we decompose G3 sequentially:
 ○ G3.2: Door Opening  
 ○ G3.1: Retrieve Dirty Dishes

• G3.2: Door Opening  
 – Type: Achieve goal (target state: door open).  
 – This sub-goal is further decomposed into two alternatives:
  • G3.2.1: Robot Door Opening  
   – Type: Perform goal (action executed by cooperating robots).  
   – Runtime: Leaf (“-”)  
  • G3.2.2: Human Door Opening  
   – Type: Perform goal (action executed by a human, e.g. companion or nurse).  
   – Runtime: Leaf (“-”)  
 – Relation: Under G3.2, the alternatives are OR because either method satisfies the door opening requirement.

• G3.1: Retrieve Dirty Dishes  
 – Type: Perform goal (action executed by the robot, possibly with cooperation).  
 – Runtime: Leaf (“-”)  
 – Relation: This task must follow door opening (sequential dependency within G3).

